module

public import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Morphisms.FinitePresentation
public import Mathlib.Algebra.Module.FinitePresentation
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Extension.Presentation.Basic
public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.AssociatedSheafScalarNormalisation
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.NaiveCotangentAffineQuasiIso
public import StacksFrontier.Topology.Sheaves.Modules.AssociatedSheaf.Quasicoherent

/-!
# Stacks 0D0K: finiteness of the naive cotangent complex of a finitely presented morphism

Stacks 0D0K.  The mathematical content is at the level of rings: if `A` is a
finitely presented `R`-algebra then `A` admits a presentation `A = R[x_1, …, x_n]/(g_1, …, g_m)`
with *both* `n` and `m` finite, and for such a presentation `P` the two-term naive cotangent
complex

`NL(P) = (I/I² ⟶ Ω_{R[x]/R} ⊗_{R[x]} A)`

has degree-`0` term free on the `n` variables — hence of finite presentation as an `A`-module —
and degree-`1` term `I/I²` generated by the classes of the `m` relations — hence of finite type.
By Stacks 00S1 this `NL(P)` is quasi-isomorphic to the canonical naive cotangent complex
`NL_{A/R}`.

On an affine open `V` of `X` mapping into an affine open `U` of `Y`, the sheafification of
`NL_{A/R}` for `R = Γ(Y, U)` and `A = Γ(X, V)` is quasi-isomorphic to `NL_{X/Y}|_V` by Stacks
0D0I, and the comparison `NL(P) ⟶ NL_{A/R}` survives sheafification: Stacks 00S1 produces not
merely a quasi-isomorphism but a *homotopy equivalence*, and a homotopy equivalence of complexes
is carried to a homotopy equivalence — hence to a quasi-isomorphism — by any additive functor.
Composing the two gives the quasi-isomorphism `(NL(P))~ ⟶ NL_{X/Y}|_V` that the tag asserts.

## Main results

* `Stacks.NaiveCotangentFinitePresentation.span_range_cotangentMk_relation`: `I/I²` is spanned
  over `A` by the classes of the relations.
* `Stacks.NaiveCotangentFinitePresentation.finite_cotangent`: for a presentation with finitely
  many relations, `I/I²` is a finite `A`-module.
* `Stacks.NaiveCotangentFinitePresentation.free_cotangentSpace` and
  `Stacks.NaiveCotangentFinitePresentation.finitePresentation_cotangentSpace`:
  `Ω_{R[x]/R} ⊗_{R[x]} A` is a free `A`-module, on the variables, and for finitely many variables
  a finitely presented `A`-module.
* `Stacks.NaiveCotangentFinitePresentation.isQuasiIso_nlMapCanonical`: the comparison chain map
  `NL(P) ⟶ NL_{A/R}` of Stacks 00S1 is a quasi-isomorphism.
* `Stacks.NaiveCotangentFinitePresentation.exists_finite_presentation_nl`: for a finitely
  presented algebra there is a finite presentation whose naive cotangent complex has degree-`0`
  term of finite presentation, degree-`1` term of finite type, and a quasi-isomorphism onto
  `NL_{A/R}`.
* `Stacks.NaiveCotangentFinitePresentation.exists_affine_finite_presentation_nl`: the same
  conclusion for `R = Γ(Y, U)` and `A = Γ(X, V)` on affine opens `V ∋ x`, `U` with
  `V ⊆ f^{-1}U`, for `f` locally of finite presentation.
* `Stacks.NaiveCotangentFinitePresentation.isFinitePresentation_associatedSheaf_cotangentSpace`,
  `Stacks.NaiveCotangentFinitePresentation.isQuasicoherent_associatedSheaf_cotangentSpace` and
  `Stacks.NaiveCotangentFinitePresentation.isQuasicoherent_associatedSheaf_cotangent`: the
  sheaves associated to the two terms are quasi-coherent, and the one associated to the
  degree-`0` term is of finite presentation.
* `Stacks.NaiveCotangentFinitePresentation.associatedSheaf_isFiniteType`: the sheaf associated to
  a finite module is of finite type, which is what the degree-`-1` term gives.
* `Stacks.NaiveCotangentFinitePresentation.isHomotopyEquiv_nlMapCanonical`: the comparison
  `NL(P) ⟶ NL_{A/R}` is a homotopy equivalence, the form of Stacks 00S1 that survives a functor.
* `Stacks.NaiveCotangentFinitePresentation.quasiIso_nlDoubleMap`: the image of that comparison
  under any additive functor out of `ModuleCat A` is a quasi-isomorphism.
* `Stacks.NaiveCotangentFinitePresentation.quasiIso_associatedNL_comp_comparisonMap`: for `V ∋ x`
  and `U` affine with `V ⊆ f^{-1}U`, the composite `(NL(P))~ ⟶ (NL_{A/R})~ ⟶ NL_{X/Y}|_V` is a
  quasi-isomorphism.
* `Stacks.NaiveCotangentFinitePresentation`
  `.exists_affine_quasiIso_associatedDouble_naiveCotangentComplex`:
  **Stacks 0D0K**, the whole statement: locally on `X` the naive cotangent complex of `f` is
  quasi-isomorphic to a two-term complex of quasi-coherent `𝒪`-modules whose degree-`0` term is
  of finite presentation and whose degree-`-1` term is of finite type.

## Implementation notes

The quasi-isomorphy of the sheafified comparison is obtained from the *homotopy* equivalence of
Stacks 00S1(4), not from any exactness of the sheafification functor.  This matters: the sheaf
associated to a module is not an exact functor on a general ringed space, so an argument by
exactness would have to pass through the stalks of `V` and their flatness over `Γ(X, V)`, while a
chain homotopy equivalence consists of morphisms and of equations between composites and sums of
composites, all of which an additive functor preserves verbatim.  The only input about
construction (3) of Stacks 01BH is therefore that it is additive, which follows from its being a
left adjoint.

`Stacks.NaiveCotangentFinitePresentation.homotopyOfIsZeroX` builds a chain homotopy between maps
of cochain complexes concentrated in degrees `-1` and `0` from a single morphism `K⁰ ⟶ L⁻¹`; the
degreewise conditions of `Homotopy` in the other degrees hold because the source vanishes there.
The two-term complexes themselves are `HomologicalComplex.double`, and the degree `-1` and `0`
terms of `double δ` are *equal* to the source and target of `δ`
(`dite_eq_left` and `Stacks.NaiveCotangentFinitePresentation.double_X_zero`), which is how the
finiteness statements about the two modules are read as statements about the two terms of the
complex of sheaves.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0D0K](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0D0K), the statement being
  worked towards.
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0D0I](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0D0I), the comparison of
  `(NL_{A/R})~` with `NL_{X/Y}|_V` on affine opens.
* [Stacks Project, Tag 00S1](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00S1), the naive cotangent
  complexes of two presentations are homotopy equivalent.
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01BH](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01BH), the sheaf associated to
  a module, and [Tag 01BD](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01BD) and
  [Tag 01BE](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01BE) for its finiteness and quasi-coherence.
-/

@[expose] public section

open Algebra AlgebraicGeometry CategoryTheory

universe u v w t

noncomputable section

namespace Stacks.NaiveCotangentFinitePresentation

variable {R : Type u} {A : Type v} [CommRing R] [CommRing A] [Algebra R A]

/-! ## Finiteness of the two terms of `NL(P)` -/

/-- **The degree-`1` term of `NL(P)` is generated by the classes of the relations.**  For any
presentation `P` of `A` over `R`, the `A`-module `I/I²` is spanned by the classes of the
polynomials `g_i` cutting out `I = ker(R[x] → A)`. -/
theorem span_range_cotangentMk_relation {ι : Type w} {σ : Type t} (P : Presentation R A ι σ) :
    Submodule.span A (Set.range fun i : σ => Extension.Cotangent.mk
        (P := P.toGenerators.toExtension)
        ⟨P.relation i, P.span_range_relation_eq_ker.le (Ideal.subset_span ⟨i, rfl⟩)⟩) = ⊤ :=
  Extension.Cotangent.span_eq_top_of_span_eq_ker (P := P.toGenerators.toExtension) P.relation
    P.span_range_relation_eq_ker

/-- **The degree-`1` term of `NL(P)` is a finite `A`-module** when the presentation `P` has
finitely many relations: the ideal `I = ker(R[x] → A)` is then generated by the relations, so
`I/I²` is spanned over `A` by their finitely many classes. -/
theorem finite_cotangent {ι : Type w} {σ : Type t} [Finite σ] (P : Presentation R A ι σ) :
    Module.Finite A P.toGenerators.toExtension.Cotangent :=
  Extension.Cotangent.finite P.fg_ker

/-- **The degree-`0` term of `NL(P)` is a free `A`-module of rank the number of variables**: it is
`Ω_{R[x]/R} ⊗_{R[x]} A`, and `Ω_{R[x]/R}` is free on the differentials `dx_i`. -/
theorem free_cotangentSpace {ι : Type w} (P : Generators R A ι) :
    Module.Free A P.toExtension.CotangentSpace :=
  .of_basis P.cotangentSpaceBasis

/-- **The degree-`0` term of `NL(P)` is a finite `A`-module** when there are finitely many
variables: it is free on the finitely many classes `dx_i ⊗ 1`. -/
theorem finite_cotangentSpace {ι : Type w} [Finite ι] (P : Generators R A ι) :
    Module.Finite A P.toExtension.CotangentSpace :=
  .of_basis P.cotangentSpaceBasis

/-- **The degree-`0` term of `NL(P)` is a finitely presented `A`-module** when there are finitely
many variables: a finite free module is finitely presented. -/
theorem finitePresentation_cotangentSpace {ι : Type w} [Finite ι] (P : Generators R A ι) :
    Module.FinitePresentation A P.toExtension.CotangentSpace :=
  have := finite_cotangentSpace P
  have := free_cotangentSpace P
  Module.finitePresentation_of_projective A _

/-! ## The comparison with the canonical naive cotangent complex -/

/-- The comparison morphism of presentations from a presentation `P` of `A` over `R` to the
canonical presentation `R[A] → A`, `[a] ↦ a`, given by the designated section. -/
def canonicalHom {ι : Type w} (P : Generators R A ι) :
    P.Hom (NaiveCotangent.canonicalGenerators R A) :=
  P.defaultHom (NaiveCotangent.canonicalGenerators R A)

/-- **Stacks 00S1 for the canonical presentation.**  The chain map
`NL(P) ⟶ NL_{A/R}` induced by `Stacks.NaiveCotangentFinitePresentation.canonicalHom` is a
quasi-isomorphism of two-term complexes of `A`-modules. -/
theorem isQuasiIso_nlMapCanonical {ι : Type w} (P : Generators R A ι) :
    NaiveCotangent.IsQuasiIso
      (NaiveCotangent.nlMap P (NaiveCotangent.canonicalGenerators R A) (canonicalHom P)) :=
  NaiveCotangent.nlMap_canonical_isQuasiIso P (canonicalHom P)

/-- **Stacks 00S1(4) for the canonical presentation.**  The chain map `NL(P) ⟶ NL_{A/R}` induced
by `Stacks.NaiveCotangentFinitePresentation.canonicalHom` is a homotopy equivalence of two-term
complexes of `A`-modules, and not merely a quasi-isomorphism: taking `R' = R` and `A' = A` in
Stacks 00S1(4), both ring maps being the identity, produces a homotopy inverse together with the
two homotopies. -/
theorem isHomotopyEquiv_nlMapCanonical {ι : Type w} (P : Generators R A ι) :
    NaiveCotangent.IsHomotopyEquiv
      (NaiveCotangent.nlMap P (NaiveCotangent.canonicalGenerators R A) (canonicalHom P)) :=
  (NaiveCotangent.nlMap_isHomotopyEquiv_and_isQuasiIso (R' := R) (S' := A)
    (by rw [Algebra.algebraMap_self]; exact Function.bijective_id)
    (by rw [Algebra.algebraMap_self]; exact Function.bijective_id)
    P (NaiveCotangent.canonicalGenerators R A) (canonicalHom P)).left

/-! ## The ring-level form of Stacks 0D0K -/

/-- **The ring-level content of Stacks 0D0K.**  If `A` is a finitely presented `R`-algebra, there
are `n, m : ℕ` and a presentation `A = R[x_1, …, x_n]/(g_1, …, g_m)` whose naive cotangent complex
`NL(P)` has degree-`0` term `Ω_{R[x]/R} ⊗_{R[x]} A` a finitely presented `A`-module, degree-`1`
term `I/I²` a finite `A`-module, and a quasi-isomorphism `NL(P) ⟶ NL_{A/R}` onto the canonical
naive cotangent complex.

Note the asymmetry: the degree-`0` term is finitely presented (indeed finite free), while the
degree-`1` term is only asserted to be of finite type. -/
theorem exists_finite_presentation_nl [Algebra.FinitePresentation R A] :
    ∃ (n m : ℕ) (P : Presentation R A (Fin n) (Fin m)),
      Module.FinitePresentation A P.toGenerators.toExtension.CotangentSpace ∧
        Module.Finite A P.toGenerators.toExtension.Cotangent ∧
        NaiveCotangent.IsQuasiIso (NaiveCotangent.nlMap P.toGenerators
          (NaiveCotangent.canonicalGenerators R A) (canonicalHom P.toGenerators)) :=
  ⟨Presentation.ofFinitePresentationVars R A, Presentation.ofFinitePresentationRels R A,
    Presentation.ofFinitePresentation R A,
    finitePresentation_cotangentSpace _, finite_cotangent _, isQuasiIso_nlMapCanonical _⟩

/-! ## Affine opens of a morphism locally of finite presentation -/

variable {X Y : Scheme.{u}}

/-- Every point `x` of `X` lies in an affine open `V` of `X` that is carried by `f` into an
affine open `U` of `Y`: choose an affine open `U` around `f x`, then an affine open `V ⊆ f^{-1}U`
around `x`. -/
theorem exists_affineOpens_le_preimage (f : X ⟶ Y) (x : X) :
    ∃ (U : Y.Opens) (V : X.Opens), IsAffineOpen U ∧ IsAffineOpen V ∧ x ∈ V ∧ V ≤ f ⁻¹ᵁ U := by
  obtain ⟨U, hU, hfU, -⟩ :=
    exists_isAffineOpen_mem_and_subset (X := Y) (x := f.base x) (U := ⊤) (by simp)
  obtain ⟨V, hV, hxV, hVU⟩ :=
    exists_isAffineOpen_mem_and_subset (X := X) (x := x) (U := f ⁻¹ᵁ U) hfU
  exact ⟨U, V, hU, hV, hxV, hVU⟩

/-- **Stacks 0D0K at the level of sections on affine opens.**  Let `f : X ⟶ Y` be locally of
finite presentation and let `x` be a point of `X`.  Then there are affine opens `V ∋ x` of `X` and
`U` of `Y` with `V ⊆ f^{-1}U` such that, writing `R = Γ(Y, U)` and `A = Γ(X, V)`, the algebra
`A` admits a presentation `A = R[x_1, …, x_n]/(g_1, …, g_m)` with `n` and `m` finite whose naive
cotangent complex `NL(P)` has degree-`0` term a finitely presented `A`-module, degree-`1` term a
finite `A`-module, and a quasi-isomorphism `NL(P) ⟶ NL_{A/R}`.

Together with Stacks 0D0I — `Stacks.NaiveCotangentAffineQuasiIso.quasiIso_comparisonMap`, which
identifies `(NL_{A/R})~` with `NL_{X/Y}|_V` in `D(𝒪_V)` for exactly such a pair `U`, `V` — this is
the content of Stacks 0D0K; what remains is to sheafify the quasi-isomorphism `NL(P) ⟶ NL_{A/R}`
produced here. -/
theorem exists_affine_finite_presentation_nl (f : X ⟶ Y) [LocallyOfFinitePresentation f] (x : X) :
    ∃ (U : Y.Opens) (V : X.Opens) (_ : IsAffineOpen U) (_ : IsAffineOpen V) (_ : x ∈ V)
      (hVU : V ≤ f ⁻¹ᵁ U),
      letI := Stacks.NaiveCotangentAffineStalk.algebraAppLE f hVU
      ∃ (n m : ℕ) (P : Presentation Γ(Y, U) Γ(X, V) (Fin n) (Fin m)),
        Module.FinitePresentation Γ(X, V) P.toGenerators.toExtension.CotangentSpace ∧
          Module.Finite Γ(X, V) P.toGenerators.toExtension.Cotangent ∧
          NaiveCotangent.IsQuasiIso (NaiveCotangent.nlMap P.toGenerators
            (NaiveCotangent.canonicalGenerators Γ(Y, U) Γ(X, V))
            (canonicalHom P.toGenerators)) := by
  obtain ⟨U, V, hU, hV, hxV, hVU⟩ := exists_affineOpens_le_preimage f x
  refine ⟨U, V, hU, hV, hxV, hVU, ?_⟩
  let := Stacks.NaiveCotangentAffineStalk.algebraAppLE f hVU
  have : Algebra.FinitePresentation Γ(Y, U) Γ(X, V) :=
    LocallyOfFinitePresentation.finitePresentation_appLE f hU hV hVU
  exact exists_finite_presentation_nl

/-! ## The sheafified terms of `NL(P)` -/

section Sheaf

open Opposite Stacks.AssociatedSheaf

attribute [local instance] hasModuleSheafify moduleSheafifyWEqualsLocallyBijective

variable (W : RingedSpace.{u, u}) {R₀ A₀ : Type u} [CommRing R₀] [CommRing A₀] [Algebra R₀ A₀]
  (α : A₀ →+* W.presheaf.obj (op ⊤))

/-- **The sheaf associated to the degree-`0` term of `NL(P)` is of finite presentation** when the
presentation `P` has finitely many variables: the degree-`0` term is a finite free `A`-module, and
the sheaf associated to a finitely presented module is of finite presentation (Stacks 01BD). -/
theorem isFinitePresentation_associatedSheaf_cotangentSpace {ι : Type u} [Finite ι]
    (P : Generators R₀ A₀ ι) :
    (associatedSheaf W α (ModuleCat.of A₀ P.toExtension.CotangentSpace)).IsFinitePresentation :=
  have := finitePresentation_cotangentSpace P
  associatedSheaf_isFinitePresentation W α _

/-- **The sheaf associated to the degree-`0` term of `NL(P)` is quasi-coherent** (Stacks 01BE). -/
theorem isQuasicoherent_associatedSheaf_cotangentSpace {ι : Type u} (P : Generators R₀ A₀ ι) :
    (associatedSheaf W α (ModuleCat.of A₀ P.toExtension.CotangentSpace)).IsQuasicoherent :=
  isQuasicoherent_associatedSheafFunctor_obj W α _

/-- **The sheaf associated to the degree-`1` term of `NL(P)` is quasi-coherent** (Stacks 01BE). -/
theorem isQuasicoherent_associatedSheaf_cotangent {ι : Type u} {σ : Type t}
    (P : Presentation R₀ A₀ ι σ) :
    (associatedSheaf W α
      (ModuleCat.of A₀ P.toGenerators.toExtension.Cotangent)).IsQuasicoherent :=
  isQuasicoherent_associatedSheafFunctor_obj W α _

end Sheaf

/-! ## Two-term complexes and additive functors -/

section TwoTermDouble

open CategoryTheory.Limits HomologicalComplex

variable {C : Type*} [Category* C] [Preadditive C]

/-- **A chain homotopy between two maps of complexes concentrated in degrees `-1` and `0`.**  For
a cochain complex `K` vanishing outside those two degrees a chain homotopy out of `K` is a single
morphism `K⁰ ⟶ L⁻¹`, and the homotopy relations in degrees `-1` and `0` are the only ones with
content: in every other degree `K` vanishes, so any two morphisms out of it agree. -/
def homotopyOfIsZeroX {K L : CochainComplex C ℤ} {φ ψ : K ⟶ L}
    (hK : ∀ i : ℤ, i ≠ -1 → i ≠ 0 → IsZero (K.X i)) (Hc : K.X 0 ⟶ L.X (-1))
    (hm : φ.f (-1) = K.d (-1) 0 ≫ Hc + ψ.f (-1))
    (h0 : φ.f 0 = Hc ≫ L.d (-1) 0 + ψ.f 0) :
    Homotopy φ ψ where
  hom i j := if h : j = -1 ∧ i = 0 then
      eqToHom (congrArg K.X h.2) ≫ Hc ≫ eqToHom (congrArg L.X h.1).symm else 0
  zero i j hij := dite_eq_right (by
    rintro ⟨rfl, rfl⟩
    exact hij (by simp [ComplexShape.up]))
  comm i := by
    by_cases e0 : i = 0
    · subst e0
      rw [dNext_eq _ (show (ComplexShape.up ℤ).Rel 0 1 by simp [ComplexShape.up]),
        prevD_eq _ (show (ComplexShape.up ℤ).Rel (-1) 0 by simp [ComplexShape.up]),
        dite_eq_right (by decide : ¬ ((0 : ℤ) = -1 ∧ (1 : ℤ) = 0)),
        dite_eq_left (show (-1 : ℤ) = -1 ∧ (0 : ℤ) = 0 from ⟨rfl, rfl⟩)]
      simp only [eqToHom_refl, Category.id_comp, Category.comp_id, comp_zero, zero_add]
      exact h0
    by_cases em : i = -1
    · subst em
      rw [dNext_eq _ (show (ComplexShape.up ℤ).Rel (-1) 0 by simp [ComplexShape.up]),
        prevD_eq _ (show (ComplexShape.up ℤ).Rel (-2) (-1) by simp [ComplexShape.up]),
        dite_eq_left (show (-1 : ℤ) = -1 ∧ (0 : ℤ) = 0 from ⟨rfl, rfl⟩),
        dite_eq_right (by decide : ¬ ((-2 : ℤ) = -1 ∧ (-1 : ℤ) = 0))]
      simp only [eqToHom_refl, Category.id_comp, Category.comp_id, zero_comp, add_zero]
      exact hm
    exact (hK i em e0).eq_of_src _ _

variable [HasZeroObject C] {M₁ M₀ N₁ N₀ : C}

/-- **The map of two-term complexes induced by a commuting square.**  A pair of morphisms
`p₁ : M₁ ⟶ N₁` and `p₀ : M₀ ⟶ N₀` commuting with `δ : M₁ ⟶ M₀` and `ε : N₁ ⟶ N₀` is a chain map
between the complexes carrying `δ` and `ε` in degrees `-1` and `0`. -/
def doubleMap (hrel : (ComplexShape.up ℤ).Rel (-1) 0) (δ : M₁ ⟶ M₀) (ε : N₁ ⟶ N₀)
    (p₁ : M₁ ⟶ N₁) (p₀ : M₀ ⟶ N₀) (hp : p₁ ≫ ε = δ ≫ p₀) :
    double δ hrel ⟶ double ε hrel :=
  mkHomFromDouble hrel (by decide) (p₁ ≫ (doubleXIso₀ ε hrel).inv)
    (p₀ ≫ (doubleXIso₁ ε hrel (by decide)).inv)
    (by
      rw [double_d ε hrel (by decide), Category.assoc, Iso.inv_hom_id_assoc, ← Category.assoc,
        hp, Category.assoc])
    (fun k _ => by rw [double_d_eq_zero₀ ε hrel 0 k (by decide), comp_zero])

/-- The degree `-1` component of `Stacks.NaiveCotangentFinitePresentation.doubleMap` is `p₁`,
read through the identifications of the two degree `-1` terms. -/
theorem doubleMap_f_negOne (hrel : (ComplexShape.up ℤ).Rel (-1) 0) (δ : M₁ ⟶ M₀) (ε : N₁ ⟶ N₀)
    (p₁ : M₁ ⟶ N₁) (p₀ : M₀ ⟶ N₀) (hp : p₁ ≫ ε = δ ≫ p₀) :
    (doubleMap hrel δ ε p₁ p₀ hp).f (-1) =
      (doubleXIso₀ δ hrel).hom ≫ p₁ ≫ (doubleXIso₀ ε hrel).inv :=
  mkHomFromDouble_f₀ _ _ _ _ _ _

/-- The degree `0` component of `Stacks.NaiveCotangentFinitePresentation.doubleMap` is `p₀`,
read through the identifications of the two degree `0` terms. -/
theorem doubleMap_f_zero (hrel : (ComplexShape.up ℤ).Rel (-1) 0) (δ : M₁ ⟶ M₀) (ε : N₁ ⟶ N₀)
    (p₁ : M₁ ⟶ N₁) (p₀ : M₀ ⟶ N₀) (hp : p₁ ≫ ε = δ ≫ p₀) :
    (doubleMap hrel δ ε p₁ p₀ hp).f 0 =
      (doubleXIso₁ δ hrel (by decide)).hom ≫ p₀ ≫ (doubleXIso₁ ε hrel (by decide)).inv :=
  mkHomFromDouble_f₁ _ _ _ _ _ _

/-- The degree `0` term of the two-term complex carrying `δ` *is* the target of `δ`. -/
theorem double_X_zero (hrel : (ComplexShape.up ℤ).Rel (-1) 0) (δ : M₁ ⟶ M₀) :
    (double δ hrel).X 0 = M₀ := by
  dsimp [double]
  rw [ite_eq_right (by decide : (0 : ℤ) ≠ -1), ite_eq_left rfl]

/-- **A homotopy equivalence of two-term complexes.**  Two chain maps `(p₁, p₀)` and `(q₁, q₀)`
in opposite directions between the complexes carrying `δ` and `ε`, together with morphisms
`H : M₀ ⟶ M₁` and `G : N₀ ⟶ N₁` exhibiting both composites as homotopic to the identity, make the
first chain map a homotopy equivalence. -/
def doubleHomotopyEquiv (hrel : (ComplexShape.up ℤ).Rel (-1) 0) (δ : M₁ ⟶ M₀) (ε : N₁ ⟶ N₀)
    (p₁ : M₁ ⟶ N₁) (p₀ : M₀ ⟶ N₀) (q₁ : N₁ ⟶ M₁) (q₀ : N₀ ⟶ M₀) (H : M₀ ⟶ M₁) (G : N₀ ⟶ N₁)
    (hp : p₁ ≫ ε = δ ≫ p₀) (hq : q₁ ≫ δ = ε ≫ q₀)
    (e₁ : p₁ ≫ q₁ = δ ≫ H + 𝟙 M₁) (e₀ : p₀ ≫ q₀ = H ≫ δ + 𝟙 M₀)
    (g₁ : q₁ ≫ p₁ = ε ≫ G + 𝟙 N₁) (g₀ : q₀ ≫ p₀ = G ≫ ε + 𝟙 N₀) :
    HomotopyEquiv (double δ hrel) (double ε hrel) where
  hom := doubleMap hrel δ ε p₁ p₀ hp
  inv := doubleMap hrel ε δ q₁ q₀ hq
  homotopyHomInvId :=
    homotopyOfIsZeroX (fun i h1 h2 => isZero_double_X δ hrel i h1 h2)
      ((doubleXIso₁ δ hrel (by decide)).hom ≫ H ≫ (doubleXIso₀ δ hrel).inv)
      (by
        simp only [comp_f, doubleMap_f_negOne, id_f, double_d δ hrel (by decide), Category.assoc,
          Iso.inv_hom_id_assoc]
        rw [← Category.assoc p₁ q₁, e₁]
        simp [Preadditive.comp_add, Preadditive.add_comp])
      (by
        simp only [comp_f, doubleMap_f_zero, id_f, double_d δ hrel (by decide), Category.assoc,
          Iso.inv_hom_id_assoc]
        rw [← Category.assoc p₀ q₀, e₀]
        simp [Preadditive.comp_add, Preadditive.add_comp])
  homotopyInvHomId :=
    homotopyOfIsZeroX (fun i h1 h2 => isZero_double_X ε hrel i h1 h2)
      ((doubleXIso₁ ε hrel (by decide)).hom ≫ G ≫ (doubleXIso₀ ε hrel).inv)
      (by
        simp only [comp_f, doubleMap_f_negOne, id_f, double_d ε hrel (by decide), Category.assoc,
          Iso.inv_hom_id_assoc]
        rw [← Category.assoc q₁ p₁, g₁]
        simp [Preadditive.comp_add, Preadditive.add_comp])
      (by
        simp only [comp_f, doubleMap_f_zero, id_f, double_d ε hrel (by decide), Category.assoc,
          Iso.inv_hom_id_assoc]
        rw [← Category.assoc q₀ p₀, g₀]
        simp [Preadditive.comp_add, Preadditive.add_comp])

end TwoTermDouble

section TwoTermDoubleMap

open CategoryTheory.Limits HomologicalComplex

variable {C : Type*} [Category* C] [Preadditive C] {M₁ M₀ N₁ N₀ : C}
  {D : Type*} [Category* D] [Preadditive D] [HasZeroObject D]

/-- **An additive functor carries a homotopy equivalence of two-term complexes to one.**  The
data of a homotopy equivalence between the complexes carrying `δ` and `ε` consists of morphisms
and of equations between composites and sums of composites, all of which an additive functor
preserves. -/
def doubleHomotopyEquivMap (hrel : (ComplexShape.up ℤ).Rel (-1) 0) (F : C ⥤ D) [F.Additive]
    (δ : M₁ ⟶ M₀) (ε : N₁ ⟶ N₀) (p₁ : M₁ ⟶ N₁) (p₀ : M₀ ⟶ N₀) (q₁ : N₁ ⟶ M₁) (q₀ : N₀ ⟶ M₀)
    (H : M₀ ⟶ M₁) (G : N₀ ⟶ N₁)
    (hp : p₁ ≫ ε = δ ≫ p₀) (hq : q₁ ≫ δ = ε ≫ q₀)
    (e₁ : p₁ ≫ q₁ = δ ≫ H + 𝟙 M₁) (e₀ : p₀ ≫ q₀ = H ≫ δ + 𝟙 M₀)
    (g₁ : q₁ ≫ p₁ = ε ≫ G + 𝟙 N₁) (g₀ : q₀ ≫ p₀ = G ≫ ε + 𝟙 N₀) :
    HomotopyEquiv (double (F.map δ) hrel) (double (F.map ε) hrel) :=
  doubleHomotopyEquiv hrel (F.map δ) (F.map ε) (F.map p₁) (F.map p₀) (F.map q₁) (F.map q₀)
    (F.map H) (F.map G)
    (by rw [← F.map_comp, ← F.map_comp, hp])
    (by rw [← F.map_comp, ← F.map_comp, hq])
    (by rw [← F.map_comp, ← F.map_id, ← F.map_comp, ← F.map_add, e₁])
    (by rw [← F.map_comp, ← F.map_id, ← F.map_comp, ← F.map_add, e₀])
    (by rw [← F.map_comp, ← F.map_id, ← F.map_comp, ← F.map_add, g₁])
    (by rw [← F.map_comp, ← F.map_id, ← F.map_comp, ← F.map_add, g₀])

/-- **A two-term chain map with a homotopy inverse becomes a quasi-isomorphism after an additive
functor.**  A homotopy equivalence is a quasi-isomorphism in any preadditive category with
homology, and an additive functor preserves the homotopy data; so the image of the chain map
carrying `(p₁, p₀)` is a quasi-isomorphism as soon as `(q₁, q₀)` is a homotopy inverse of
`(p₁, p₀)` before the functor is applied. -/
theorem quasiIso_doubleMap_map [CategoryWithHomology D]
    (hrel : (ComplexShape.up ℤ).Rel (-1) 0) (F : C ⥤ D) [F.Additive]
    (δ : M₁ ⟶ M₀) (ε : N₁ ⟶ N₀) (p₁ : M₁ ⟶ N₁) (p₀ : M₀ ⟶ N₀) (q₁ : N₁ ⟶ M₁) (q₀ : N₀ ⟶ M₀)
    (H : M₀ ⟶ M₁) (G : N₀ ⟶ N₁)
    (hp : p₁ ≫ ε = δ ≫ p₀) (hq : q₁ ≫ δ = ε ≫ q₀)
    (e₁ : p₁ ≫ q₁ = δ ≫ H + 𝟙 M₁) (e₀ : p₀ ≫ q₀ = H ≫ δ + 𝟙 M₀)
    (g₁ : q₁ ≫ p₁ = ε ≫ G + 𝟙 N₁) (g₀ : q₀ ≫ p₀ = G ≫ ε + 𝟙 N₀)
    (hpF : F.map p₁ ≫ F.map ε = F.map δ ≫ F.map p₀) :
    QuasiIso (doubleMap hrel (F.map δ) (F.map ε) (F.map p₁) (F.map p₀) hpF) :=
  (doubleHomotopyEquivMap hrel F δ ε p₁ p₀ q₁ q₀ H G hp hq e₁ e₀ g₁ g₀).quasiIso_hom

end TwoTermDoubleMap

/-! ## The two-term complex `NL(P)` read in `ModuleCat` -/

section NLModule

open CategoryTheory.Limits HomologicalComplex

variable {R₀ A₀ : Type u} [CommRing R₀] [CommRing A₀] [Algebra R₀ A₀]

/-- The degree `-1` term `I/I²` of `NL(P)`, read as an object of `ModuleCat A₀`. -/
abbrev nlMod₁ {ι : Type u} (P : Generators R₀ A₀ ι) : ModuleCat.{u} A₀ :=
  ModuleCat.of A₀ (NaiveCotangent.nlRestrict A₀ P).X₁

/-- The degree `0` term `Ω_{R₀[x]/R₀} ⊗ A₀` of `NL(P)`, read as an object of `ModuleCat A₀`. -/
abbrev nlMod₀ {ι : Type u} (P : Generators R₀ A₀ ι) : ModuleCat.{u} A₀ :=
  ModuleCat.of A₀ (NaiveCotangent.nlRestrict A₀ P).X₀

/-- The differential of `NL(P)`, read as a morphism of `ModuleCat A₀`. -/
abbrev nlModD {ι : Type u} (P : Generators R₀ A₀ ι) : nlMod₁ P ⟶ nlMod₀ P :=
  ModuleCat.ofHom (NaiveCotangent.nlRestrict A₀ P).d

/-- The degree `-1` component of the comparison chain map `NL(P) ⟶ NL_{A₀/R₀}`, read in
`ModuleCat A₀`. -/
abbrev nlModMap₁ {ι : Type u} (P : Generators R₀ A₀ ι) :
    nlMod₁ P ⟶ nlMod₁ (NaiveCotangent.canonicalGenerators R₀ A₀) :=
  ModuleCat.ofHom (NaiveCotangent.nlMap P (NaiveCotangent.canonicalGenerators R₀ A₀)
    (canonicalHom P)).f₁

/-- The degree `0` component of the comparison chain map `NL(P) ⟶ NL_{A₀/R₀}`, read in
`ModuleCat A₀`. -/
abbrev nlModMap₀ {ι : Type u} (P : Generators R₀ A₀ ι) :
    nlMod₀ P ⟶ nlMod₀ (NaiveCotangent.canonicalGenerators R₀ A₀) :=
  ModuleCat.ofHom (NaiveCotangent.nlMap P (NaiveCotangent.canonicalGenerators R₀ A₀)
    (canonicalHom P)).f₀

/-- The comparison chain map `NL(P) ⟶ NL_{A₀/R₀}` commutes with the two differentials: this is
the chain map condition of `NaiveCotangent.ChainMap`, read in `ModuleCat A₀`. -/
theorem nlModMap_comm {ι : Type u} (P : Generators R₀ A₀ ι) :
    nlModMap₁ P ≫ nlModD (NaiveCotangent.canonicalGenerators R₀ A₀) =
      nlModD P ≫ nlModMap₀ P :=
  ModuleCat.hom_ext (NaiveCotangent.nlMap P (NaiveCotangent.canonicalGenerators R₀ A₀)
    (canonicalHom P)).comm

variable {D : Type*} [Category* D] [Preadditive D] [HasZeroObject D]

/-- **The comparison `NL(P) ⟶ NL_{A₀/R₀}`, pushed through an additive functor.**  For an additive
functor `F` out of `ModuleCat A₀` — in the application, the sheaf associated to a module of
Stacks 01BH — this is the induced chain map of the two complexes carrying `F(d_P)` and
`F(d_can)` in degrees `-1` and `0`. -/
def nlDoubleMap {ι : Type u} (P : Generators R₀ A₀ ι) (F : ModuleCat.{u} A₀ ⥤ D) [F.Additive]
    (hrel : (ComplexShape.up ℤ).Rel (-1) 0) :
    double (F.map (nlModD P)) hrel ⟶
      double (F.map (nlModD (NaiveCotangent.canonicalGenerators R₀ A₀))) hrel :=
  doubleMap hrel _ _ (F.map (nlModMap₁ P)) (F.map (nlModMap₀ P))
    (by rw [← F.map_comp, ← F.map_comp, nlModMap_comm])

/-- A linear map `b ∘ a` that is the identity up to a factorisation `d ∘ c` gives the relation
`a ≫ b = c ≫ d + 𝟙` of homotopy data in `ModuleCat`. -/
private theorem ofHom_comp_ofHom_eq_add_id {B : Type u} [CommRing B] {M N P : Type u}
    [AddCommGroup M] [Module B M] [AddCommGroup N] [Module B N] [AddCommGroup P] [Module B P]
    (a : M →ₗ[B] N) (b : N →ₗ[B] M) (c : M →ₗ[B] P) (d : P →ₗ[B] M)
    (h : b ∘ₗ a - LinearMap.id = d ∘ₗ c) :
    ModuleCat.ofHom a ≫ ModuleCat.ofHom b = ModuleCat.ofHom c ≫ ModuleCat.ofHom d + 𝟙 (.of B M) :=
  ModuleCat.hom_ext (by simpa [sub_eq_iff_eq_add] using h)

/-- **Stacks 00S1 survives any additive functor.**  The comparison chain map
`NL(P) ⟶ NL_{A₀/R₀}` is a homotopy equivalence of two-term complexes of `A₀`-modules, so its
image under an additive functor is a homotopy equivalence, hence a quasi-isomorphism.  No
exactness of the functor is used: only that it preserves composition, sums and identities, which
is all the homotopy data consists of. -/
theorem quasiIso_nlDoubleMap [CategoryWithHomology D] {ι : Type u} (P : Generators R₀ A₀ ι)
    (F : ModuleCat.{u} A₀ ⥤ D) [F.Additive] (hrel : (ComplexShape.up ℤ).Rel (-1) 0) :
    QuasiIso (nlDoubleMap P F hrel) := by
  obtain ⟨g, ⟨h, hh₁, hh₀⟩, ⟨k, hk₁, hk₀⟩⟩ := isHomotopyEquiv_nlMapCanonical P
  refine quasiIso_doubleMap_map hrel F (nlModD P)
    (nlModD (NaiveCotangent.canonicalGenerators R₀ A₀)) (nlModMap₁ P) (nlModMap₀ P)
    (ModuleCat.ofHom g.f₁) (ModuleCat.ofHom g.f₀) (ModuleCat.ofHom h) (ModuleCat.ofHom k)
    (nlModMap_comm P) (ModuleCat.hom_ext g.comm) ?_ ?_ ?_ ?_ _
  · exact ofHom_comp_ofHom_eq_add_id _ _ _ _ (by simpa using hh₁)
  · exact ofHom_comp_ofHom_eq_add_id _ _ _ _ (by simpa using hh₀)
  · exact ofHom_comp_ofHom_eq_add_id _ _ _ _ (by simpa using hk₁)
  · exact ofHom_comp_ofHom_eq_add_id _ _ _ _ (by simpa using hk₀)

end NLModule

/-! ## The sheaf associated to a finite module is of finite type -/

section FiniteType

open CategoryTheory.Limits Opposite Stacks.AssociatedSheaf

attribute [local instance] hasModuleSheafify moduleSheafifyWEqualsLocallyBijective

/-- **A finite module has a presentation with finitely many generators.**  Take a finite
generating family and the tautological family of relations, indexed by the whole kernel of the
map from the free module on the generators: that kernel is its own span, so the two conditions
characterising a presentation hold.  No finiteness of the relations is asserted, and for a
module that is merely finite none is available. -/
theorem exists_presentation_finite_generators (A₀ : Type u) [CommRing A₀] (M : Type u)
    [AddCommGroup M] [Module A₀ M] [Module.Finite A₀ M] :
    ∃ Q : Module.Presentation.{u, u} A₀ M, Finite Q.G := by
  classical
  obtain ⟨G : Type u, _, var, hG⟩ := Submodule.fg_iff_exists_finite_generating_family.1
    (Module.finite_def.1 (inferInstance : Module.Finite A₀ M))
  refine ⟨{ G := G
            R := LinearMap.ker (Finsupp.linearCombination A₀ var)
            relation := Subtype.val
            var := var
            linearCombination_var_relation := fun r => r.2
            toIsPresentation := Module.Relations.Solution.isPresentation_mk _ hG ?_ }, ‹_›⟩
  exact (Submodule.span_eq _).symm.trans (congrArg (Submodule.span A₀) Subtype.range_coe.symm)

/-- **Stacks 01BH and 01BD.**  The sheaf of `𝒪_W`-modules associated to a *finite* `A₀`-module is
of finite type.  Construction (3) of Stacks 01BH is a left adjoint, so it carries the cokernel
presentation of the module to a presentation of the associated sheaf; the generators of that
presentation are indexed by the generators of the module, finitely many, and the trivial cover
turns a global family of generators into local generating data. -/
theorem associatedSheaf_isFiniteType (W : RingedSpace.{u, u}) {A₀ : Type u} [CommRing A₀]
    (α : A₀ →+* W.presheaf.obj (op ⊤)) (N : ModuleCat.{u} A₀) [Module.Finite A₀ N] :
    (associatedSheaf W α N).IsFiniteType := by
  classical
  obtain ⟨Q, hG⟩ := exists_presentation_finite_generators A₀ N
  have := hG
  refine SheafOfModules.IsFiniteType.mk.{u, u, u} ?_
  refine ⟨(sheafPresentation.{u} W α Q).quasicoherentData.localGeneratorsData, ?_⟩
  constructor
  intro i
  exact ⟨by
    change Finite (sheafPresentation.{u} W α Q).generators.I
    exact hG⟩

end FiniteType

/-! ## The sheafified comparison on an affine open -/

section AffineSheaf

open CategoryTheory.Limits HomologicalComplex Opposite Stacks.AssociatedSheaf
open Stacks.NaiveCotangentAffineIso Stacks.NaiveCotangentAffineMap

attribute [local instance] hasModuleSheafify moduleSheafifyWEqualsLocallyBijective
attribute [local instance] preservesBinaryBiproducts_of_preservesBinaryCoproducts

/-- Construction (3) of Stacks 01BH is a left adjoint, which is clause (4) of the same tag. -/
local instance isLeftAdjoint_associatedSheafFunctor (W : RingedSpace.{u, u}) {A₀ : Type u}
    [CommRing A₀] (α : A₀ →+* W.presheaf.obj (op ⊤)) :
    (associatedSheafFunctor.{u, u} W α).IsLeftAdjoint :=
  (associatedSheafAdjunction.{u, u} W α).isLeftAdjoint

/-- **The complex of sheaves of `𝒪_V`-modules associated to a two-term complex of
`Γ(X, V)`-modules**: construction (3) of Stacks 01BH applied to the differential `δ`, with the
sheaf associated to the source in degree `-1` and the sheaf associated to the target in degree
`0`.  For `δ` the differential of `NL_{A/R}` this is
`Stacks.NaiveCotangentAffineIso.associatedNaiveCotangentComplex`. -/
abbrev associatedDouble {V : X.Opens} {M₁ M₀ : ModuleCat.{u} Γ(X, V)} (δ : M₁ ⟶ M₀) :
    CochainComplex V.toScheme.Modules ℤ :=
  double ((associatedSheafFunctor.{u, u} V.toScheme.toRingedSpace (alpha (V := V))).map δ)
    (show (ComplexShape.up ℤ).Rel (-1) 0 from by decide)

/-- **The sheafified comparison `(NL(P))~ ⟶ NL_{X/Y}|_V` is a quasi-isomorphism.**  Let `U` be an
affine open of `Y`, let `V` be an affine open of `X` with `V ⊆ f^{-1}U`, write `R = Γ(Y, U)` and
`A = Γ(X, V)`, and let `P` be any presentation of `A` over `R`.  Then the complex of sheaves of
`𝒪_V`-modules associated to `NL(P)` by construction (3) of Stacks 01BH is quasi-isomorphic to the
restriction of `NL_{X/Y}` to `V`, by the composite of the sheafification of the comparison
`NL(P) ⟶ NL_{A/R}` of Stacks 00S1 with the canonical map of Stacks 0D0I.
Affineness of `U` and `V` is used only through Stacks 0D0I. -/
theorem quasiIso_associatedNL_comp_comparisonMap {ι : Type u} (f : X ⟶ Y) {U : Y.Opens}
    {V : X.Opens} (hVU : V ≤ f ⁻¹ᵁ U) (hU : IsAffineOpen U) (hV : IsAffineOpen V)
    (P : letI := Stacks.NaiveCotangentAffineStalk.algebraAppLE f hVU
      Generators Γ(Y, U) Γ(X, V) ι) :
    letI := Stacks.NaiveCotangentAffineStalk.algebraAppLE f hVU
    QuasiIso (nlDoubleMap P
        (associatedSheafFunctor.{u, u} V.toScheme.toRingedSpace (alpha (V := V)))
        (show (ComplexShape.up ℤ).Rel (-1) 0 from by decide) ≫ comparisonMap f hVU) := by
  let := Stacks.NaiveCotangentAffineStalk.algebraAppLE f hVU
  have h₁ := quasiIso_nlDoubleMap P
    (associatedSheafFunctor.{u, u} V.toScheme.toRingedSpace (alpha (V := V)))
    (show (ComplexShape.up ℤ).Rel (-1) 0 from by decide)
  have h₂ := Stacks.NaiveCotangentAffineQuasiIso.quasiIso_comparisonMap V f hVU hU hV
  exact quasiIso_comp (hφ := h₁) (hφ' := h₂) _ _

/-- **Stacks 0D0K.**  Let `f : X ⟶ Y` be locally of finite presentation and let `x` be a point of
`X`.  Then there are affine opens `V ∋ x` of `X` and `U` of `Y` with `V ⊆ f^{-1}U`, a two-term
complex `δ : M₁ ⟶ M₀` of `Γ(X, V)`-modules, and a quasi-isomorphism from the complex of sheaves
of `𝒪_V`-modules associated to `δ` to the restriction of `NL_{X/Y}` to `V`, such that the
degree-`0` term of that complex of sheaves is quasi-coherent of finite presentation and its
degree-`-1` term is quasi-coherent of finite type.

The two-term complex is `NL(P)` for a presentation `A = R[x_1, …, x_n]/(g_1, …, g_m)` of
`A = Γ(X, V)` over `R = Γ(Y, U)` with finitely many variables and finitely many relations, which
exists because `f` is locally of finite presentation.  Its degree-`0` term is finite free on the
variables, hence finitely presented, and its degree-`-1` term `I/I²` is generated by the classes
of the relations, hence finite — and no better, which is why the two degrees are given different
finiteness assertions.  The quasi-isomorphism is the sheafification of the comparison
`NL(P) ⟶ NL_{A/R}` of Stacks 00S1, which is a homotopy equivalence and therefore survives any
additive functor, followed by the canonical map `(NL_{A/R})~ ⟶ NL_{X/Y}|_V` of Stacks 0D0I. -/
@[stacks 0D0K "the whole statement.  `locally on X` is read as: for every point `x` of `X` there \
are affine opens `V ∋ x` of `X` and `U` of `Y` with `V ⊆ f^{-1}U`, and the complex of \
quasi-coherent `O_V`-modules is produced on that `V`.  The source says `NL_{X/Y}` is \
quasi-isomorphic TO the two-term complex; what is produced here is a quasi-isomorphism FROM the \
two-term complex to `NL_{X/Y}|_V`, which is the same content, a quasi-isomorphism being \
invertible in `D(O_V)`.  The two-term complex is `NL(P)` sheafified, for a presentation of \
`Gamma(X, V)` over `Gamma(Y, U)` with finitely many variables and finitely many relations, which \
exists because `f` is locally of finite presentation; its degree-0 term is finite free on the \
variables and its degree-(-1) term `I/I^2` is generated by the classes of the relations and no \
better, which is why the two degrees are given DIFFERENT finiteness assertions -- degree 0 \
`IsFinitePresentation`, degree -1 `IsFiniteType` -- exactly as the tag states.  The \
quasi-isomorphism is the sheafification of the comparison `NL(P) -> NL_{A/R}` of \
[Stacks, Tag 00S1], taken in its HOMOTOPY EQUIVALENCE form so that it survives an additive \
functor, composed with the canonical map `(NL_{A/R})~ -> NL_{X/Y}|_V` of [Stacks, Tag 0D0I]; the \
target is `Scheme.naiveCotangentComplex f` ([Stacks, Tag 0D0H]) restricted along `V.iota`.  \
The sole hypothesis is `[LocallyOfFinitePresentation f]`, which is the source's own: \
[Stacks, Tag 0D0K] is the lemma for a morphism locally of finite presentation.  Every other \
input is proved: [Stacks, Tag 00S1] in `isHomotopyEquiv_nlMapCanonical`, [Stacks, Tag 0D0I] in \
`Stacks.NaiveCotangentAffineQuasiIso.quasiIso_comparisonMap`, and the finiteness of the two \
terms in `finitePresentation_cotangentSpace` and `finite_cotangent`"]
theorem exists_affine_quasiIso_associatedDouble_naiveCotangentComplex (f : X ⟶ Y)
    [LocallyOfFinitePresentation f] (x : X) :
    ∃ (U : Y.Opens) (V : X.Opens) (_ : IsAffineOpen U) (_ : IsAffineOpen V) (_ : x ∈ V)
      (_ : V ≤ f ⁻¹ᵁ U) (M₁ M₀ : ModuleCat.{u} Γ(X, V)) (δ : M₁ ⟶ M₀)
      (ψ : associatedDouble δ ⟶ restrictedNaiveCotangentComplex f (V := V)),
      QuasiIso ψ ∧ ((associatedDouble δ).X 0).IsFinitePresentation ∧
        ((associatedDouble δ).X (-1)).IsFiniteType ∧
        ((associatedDouble δ).X 0).IsQuasicoherent ∧
        ((associatedDouble δ).X (-1)).IsQuasicoherent := by
  obtain ⟨U, V, hU, hV, hxV, hVU⟩ := exists_affineOpens_le_preimage f x
  let := Stacks.NaiveCotangentAffineStalk.algebraAppLE f hVU
  have : Algebra.FinitePresentation Γ(Y, U) Γ(X, V) :=
    LocallyOfFinitePresentation.finitePresentation_appLE f hU hV hVU
  let P : Presentation Γ(Y, U) Γ(X, V) (ULift.{u} (Fin
      (Presentation.ofFinitePresentationVars Γ(Y, U) Γ(X, V)))) (ULift.{u} (Fin
      (Presentation.ofFinitePresentationRels Γ(Y, U) Γ(X, V)))) :=
    (Presentation.ofFinitePresentation Γ(Y, U) Γ(X, V)).reindex Equiv.ulift Equiv.ulift
  have hX : (associatedDouble (nlModD P.toGenerators)).X (-1) =
      (associatedSheafFunctor.{u, u} V.toScheme.toRingedSpace (alpha (V := V))).obj
        (nlMod₁ P.toGenerators) := dite_eq_left rfl
  refine ⟨U, V, hU, hV, hxV, hVU, nlMod₁ P.toGenerators, nlMod₀ P.toGenerators,
    nlModD P.toGenerators, nlDoubleMap P.toGenerators
